{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast.","source_span":"Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes.","source_span":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"The most common type of breast cancer is ductal carcinoma, which begins in the cells of the ducts.","source_span":"The most common type of breast cancer is ductal carcinoma, which begins in the cells of the ducts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Lobular carcinoma, which begins in the lobes or lobules, is more often found in both breasts than are other types of breast cancer.","source_span":"Cancer that begins in the lobes or lobules is called lobular carcinoma and is more often found in both breasts than are other types of breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Inflammatory breast cancer is an uncommon type of breast cancer in which the breast is warm, red, and swollen.","source_span":"Inflammatory breast cancer is an uncommon type of breast cancer in which the breast is warm, red, and swollen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"A family history of breast cancer in a first-degree relative (mother, daughter, or sister) is a risk factor for breast cancer.","source_span":"A family history of breast cancer in a first-degree relative (mother, daughter, or sister)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Inherited changes in the BRCA1 or BRCA2 genes increase the risk of breast cancer.","source_span":"Inherited changes in the  BRCA1  or  BRCA2  genes or in other genes that increase the risk of breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool estimates a woman's risk of breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90.","source_span":"NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool uses a woman's risk factors to estimate her risk for breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"For more information on breast cancer risk, one can call 1-800-4-CANCER.","source_span":"For more information on breast cancer risk, call 1-800-4-CANCER."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Protective factors that decrease breast cancer risk include estrogen-only hormone therapy after a hysterectomy, selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs), and aromatase inhibitors.","source_span":"Estrogen-only hormone therapy after a hysterectomy.       -  Selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs).        -  Aromatase inhibitors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Hereditary breast cancer makes up about 5% to 10% of all breast cancer.","source_span":"Hereditary breast cancer makes up about 5% to 10% of all breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","statement":"Signs of breast cancer include a lump or thickening near the breast or underarm, a change in breast size or shape, skin dimpling, an inward-turned nipple, bloody nipple discharge, and dimples resembling orange peel (peau d'orange).","source_span":"Dimples in the breast that look like the skin of an orange, called peau dorange."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the most common type of breast cancer and where does it begin?","answer":"The most common type of breast cancer is ductal carcinoma, which begins in the cells of the ducts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How many lobes does each breast typically have?","answer":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What percentage of all breast cancer cases are hereditary?","answer":"Hereditary breast cancer makes up about 5% to 10% of all breast cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What phone number can someone call for more information on breast cancer risk?","answer":"They can call 1-800-4-CANCER."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What time period and age does NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool estimate risk for?","answer":"It estimates a woman's risk for breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What medicines are protective factors that decrease breast cancer risk?","answer":"Estrogen-only hormone therapy after a hysterectomy, selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs), and aromatase inhibitors decrease breast cancer risk."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Male breast cancer is usually detected in men between 60 and 70 years of age.","source_span":"it is usually detected (found) in men between 60 and 70 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Male breast cancer makes up less than 1% of all cases of breast cancer.","source_span":"Male breast cancer makes up less than 1% of all cases of breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Most men with breast cancer have infiltrating ductal carcinoma, cancer that has spread beyond the cells lining ducts in the breast.","source_span":"Infiltrating ductal carcinoma: Cancer that has spread beyond the cells lining ducts in the breast. Most men with breast cancer have this type of cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Lobular carcinoma in situ, which sometimes occurs in women, has not been seen in men.","source_span":"Lobular carcinoma in situ (abnormal cells found in one of the lobes or sections of the breast), which sometimes occurs in women, has not been seen in men."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Risk factors for breast cancer in men include radiation exposure, diseases linked to high estrogen levels such as cirrhosis or Klinefelter syndrome, and having several female relatives with breast cancer, especially with a BRCA2 gene alteration.","source_span":"Being exposed to radiation.     - Having a disease linked to high levels of estrogen in the body, such as cirrhosis (liver disease) or Klinefelter syndrome (a genetic disorder.)    - Having several female relatives who have had breast cancer, especially relatives who have an alteration of the BRCA2 gene."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Hereditary breast cancer makes up about 5% to 10% of all breast cancer.","source_span":"Hereditary breast cancer makes up about 5% to 10% of all breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Paget disease of the nipple is a tumor that has grown from ducts beneath the nipple onto the surface of the nipple.","source_span":"Paget disease of the nipple: A tumor that has grown from ducts beneath the nipple onto the surface of the nipple."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"Survival for men with breast cancer is similar to survival for women with breast cancer.","source_span":"Survival for men with breast cancer is similar to survival for women with breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"The estrogen and progesterone receptor test measures the amount of hormone receptors in cancer tissue to show whether hormone therapy may stop the cancer from growing.","source_span":"A test to measure the amount of estrogen and progesterone (hormones) receptors in cancer tissue...The test results show whether hormone therapy may stop the cancer from growing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","statement":"The HER2 test measures the amount of HER2, a growth factor protein that sends growth signals to cells, in cancer tissue.","source_span":"HER2 test: A test to measure the amount of HER2 in cancer tissue. HER2 is a growth factor protein that sends growth signals to cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[0],"question":"At what age range is male breast cancer usually detected?","answer":"Male breast cancer is usually detected in men between 60 and 70 years of age."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What percentage of all breast cancer cases does male breast cancer represent?","answer":"Male breast cancer makes up less than 1% of all cases of breast cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What type of breast cancer do most men with breast cancer have?","answer":"Most men with breast cancer have infiltrating ductal carcinoma, cancer that has spread beyond the cells lining the breast's ducts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the risk factors for breast cancer in men?","answer":"Risk factors include radiation exposure, diseases linked to high estrogen levels such as cirrhosis or Klinefelter syndrome, and having several female relatives with breast cancer, especially those with a BRCA2 gene alteration."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is Paget disease of the nipple?","answer":"It is a tumor that has grown from ducts beneath the nipple onto the surface of the nipple."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How does survival for men with breast cancer compare to survival for women with breast cancer?","answer":"Survival for men with breast cancer is similar to survival for women with breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Breast cancer occurs about once in every 3,000 pregnancies.","source_span":"Breast cancer occurs about once in every 3,000 pregnancies."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Breast cancer during pregnancy occurs most often between the ages of 32 and 38.","source_span":"It occurs most often between the ages of 32 and 38."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Lactation and breast-feeding should be stopped if surgery or chemotherapy is planned.","source_span":"Lactation (breast milk production) and breast-feeding should be stopped if surgery or chemotherapy is planned."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Anticancer drugs, especially cyclophosphamide and methotrexate, may occur in high levels in breast milk and may harm the nursing baby.","source_span":"Many anticancer drugs, especially cyclophosphamide and methotrexate, may occur in high levels in breast milk and may harm the nursing baby."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Breast cancer cells do not seem to pass from the mother to the unborn baby.","source_span":"Breast cancer cells do not seem to pass from the mother to the unborn baby."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Some doctors recommend that a woman wait 2 years after treatment for breast cancer before trying to have a baby, so that any early return of the cancer would be detected.","source_span":"some doctors recommend that a woman wait 2 years after treatment for breast cancer before trying to have a baby, so that any early return of the cancer would be detected."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Pregnancy does not seem to affect the survival of women who have had breast cancer in the past.","source_span":"Pregnancy does not seem to affect the survival of women who have had breast cancer in the past."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","statement":"Breast exams should be part of prenatal and postnatal care.","source_span":"Breast exams should be part of prenatal and postnatal care."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How often does breast cancer occur during pregnancy?","answer":"Breast cancer occurs about once in every 3,000 pregnancies."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Between what ages does pregnancy-associated breast cancer occur most often?","answer":"It occurs most often between the ages of 32 and 38."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Why should breast-feeding be stopped if chemotherapy is planned for a woman with breast cancer?","answer":"Breast-feeding should be stopped because many anticancer drugs, especially cyclophosphamide and methotrexate, may occur in high levels in breast milk and may harm the nursing baby."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Can breast cancer cells pass from a pregnant mother to her unborn baby?","answer":"No, breast cancer cells do not seem to pass from the mother to the unborn baby."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How long do some doctors recommend a woman wait after breast cancer treatment before trying to have a baby?","answer":"Some doctors recommend waiting 2 years after treatment for breast cancer before trying to have a baby, so any early return of the cancer would be detected."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Does pregnancy affect the survival of women who have previously had breast cancer?","answer":"No, pregnancy does not seem to affect the survival of women who have had breast cancer in the past."}
